ack-relation field whose name changed — that's a public field name your application code accesses via `.include()`/`.select()`/the generated TypeScript types, so update those call sites to the corrected name.\nnode_modules/@prisma/orm-postgres/skills/prisma-8/upgrading/app/upgrades/0.16-to-0.17/instructions.md:276:      relation `.include()` over a date column now decodes instead of failing with\nnode_modules/@prisma/orm-postgres/skills/prisma-8/upgrading/app/upgrades/0.16-to-0.17/instructions.md:429:      `.include()`'s nested rows, an aggregated child row set — projects each column through its\nnode_modules/@prisma/orm-postgres/skills/prisma-8/upgrading/app/upgrades/0.16-to-0.17/instructions.md:499:      A `sqlite/json@1` column read through a nested `.include()` arrives as a parsed document\nnode_modules/@prisma/orm-postgres/skills/prisma-8/upgrading/app/upgrades/8.0.0-rc.21-to-8.0.0-rc.22/instructions.md:134:      `createMongoRuntime()` takes `{ context, adapter, driver }`, as the SQL runtime does: create the adapter with `stack.adapter.create(stack)` and pass it beside the context. The runtime reads the target from `context.contract.target`, so build the context over the validated contract. `MongoExecutionContext` no longer has a `stack` member or a second type parameter, and its contract must be a MongoDB contract, as SQL's `ExecutionContext` requires a SQL contract: write `MongoExecutionContext<Contract>`, and build it with `createMongoExecutionContext({ contract, stack })` over a validated contract. `createMongoExecutionStack()` still takes an optional `driver`; given one, it returns `MongoExecutionStackWithDriver`, whose `driver` is always set, and without one it returns `MongoExecutionStack`, which has no `driver` member, as for SQL. `buildMongoEnums()` is removed: read the contract's enum accessors from `db.enums`, from `mongo({ contract })` or `mongoStatic({ contract })`. `MongoExecutionContext` has no `enums` member. The detection matches files that call `createMongoRuntime(`, read `.stack` from a context, give `MongoExecutionContext` two type arguments or a non-contract type argument, or name `buildMongoEnums` or `MongoEnumAccessors`.\nnode_modules/@prisma/orm-postgres/skills/prisma-8/upgrading/app/upgrades/8.0.0-rc.21-to-8.0.0-rc.22/instructions.md:463:const driver = stack.driver.create();\nnode_modules/@prisma/orm-postgres/skills/prisma-8/upgrading/app/upgrades/8.0.0-rc.21-to-8.0.0-rc.22/instructions.md:467:Replace `MongoDriverImpl.fromDb(client.db(dbName))` with `driver.connect({ kind: 'mongoClient', client, dbName })` on a driver created the same way. Code that only sends DDL through a control adapter can use the control driver instead: `await mongoControlDriver.create(url)`, with `mongoControlDriver` the default export of `@prisma/orm-mongo/driver/control`. The control driver uses the database the URL names, so put `dbName` in the URL's path, for example `mongodb://localhost:27017/app`.\nnode_modules/@prisma/orm-postgres/skills/prisma-8/upgrading/app/upgrades/8.0.0-rc.21-to-8.0.0-rc.22/instructions.md:512:const runtime = createMongoRuntime({ context, adapter: stack.adapter.create(stack), driver });\nnode_modules/@prisma/orm-postgres/skills/prisma-8/upgrading/app/upgrades/8.0.0-rc.18-to-8.0.0-rc.19/instructions.md:263:      On a client that closes at once (`postgres({ contract, url })`, `sqlite()`, `supabase()`), an ORM call that resolves to one value, such as `first()`, `create()`, `include(...).create()` or a nested create, starts when it is called, and `close()` waits for it, so one left pending at `close()` now completes instead of rejecting: a `create()` used to reject with \"Runtime is closed\", and `include(...).create()` and a nested create with \"Postgres client is closed\". A streaming read such as `all()` starts when it is read; read after `close()`, it rejects with \"Postgres client is closed\" instead of \"Runtime is closed\". Update code or tests that expect the old outcome.\nnode_modules/@prisma/orm-postgres/skills/prisma-8/upgrading/app/upgrades/8.0.0-rc.18-to-8.0.0-rc.19/instructions.md:270:      A contract built with `defineContract` from TypeScript now types each relation with its target model, namespace and cardinality, as an emitted `contract.d.ts` does. Rows from `include(...)`, which were typed `unknown` and always as an array, now have the target model's field types: a has-many or many-to-many relation is an array, a belongs-to relation is one row (or `null` when optional), and a has-one relation is one row or `null`. A cast on an included value that disagrees with this type now fails to typecheck. The detection matches a file that imports `defineContract` from a SQL contract builder; the change matters only where code queries through the contract it builds without an emitted `contract.d.ts`.\nnode_modules/@prisma/orm-postgres/skills/prisma-8/upgrading/app/upgrades/8.0.0-rc.18-to-8.0.0-rc.19/instructions.md:1026:await db.orm.public.Post.create({ title: 'Hello', publishedAt: new Date() as unknown as string });\nnode_modules/@prisma/orm-postgres/skills/prisma-8/upgrading/app/upgrades/8.0.0-rc.18-to-8.0.0-rc.19/instructions.md:1029:await db.orm.public.Post.create({ title: 'Hello', publishedAt: new Date().toISOString() });\nnode_modules/@prisma/orm-postgres/skills/prisma-8/upgrading/app/upgrades/8.0.0-rc.18-to-8.0.0-rc.19/instructions.md:1377:  const post = await db.orm.Post.include('author').first();\nnode_modules/@prisma/orm-postgres/skills/prisma-8/upgrading/app/upgrades/8.0.0-rc.18-to-8.0.0-rc.19/instructions.md:1398:The same holds for reducers inside `include(...)`, such as `posts.count()`. The aggregates come from the aggregate descriptors of the target, the adapter and every extension, as `prisma contract emit` collects them. Aggregates contributed only by the execution stack, not by a pack in the contract, are still not in the contract's types and still need the cast.\nnode_modules/@prisma/orm-postgres/skills/prisma-8/upgrading/app/upgrades/8.0.0-rc.18-to-8.0.0-rc.19/instructions.md:1426:- await db.orm.User.create({ id: '123e4567-e89b-12d3-a456-426614174000' as Char<36>, email });\nnode_modules/@prisma/orm-postgres/skills/prisma-8/upgrading/app/upgrades/8.0.0-rc.18-to-8.0.0-rc.19/instructions.md:1428:+ await db.orm.User.create({ id: '123e4567-e89b-12d3-a456-426614174000', email });\nnode_modules/@prisma/orm-postgres/skills/prisma-8/upgrading/app/upgrades/8.0.0-rc.18-to-8.0.0-rc.19/instructions.md:1550:const user = await db.orm.users.create({ name: 'Alice' });\nnode_modules/@prisma/orm-postgres/skills/prisma-8/upgrading/app/upgrades/8.0.0-rc.18-to-8.0.0-rc.19/instructions.md:1552:const posts = await db.orm.posts.where({ authorId: user._id }).include('author').all();\nnode_modules/@prisma/orm-postgres/skills/prisma-8/upgrading/app/upgrades/8.0.0-rc.18-to-8.0.0-rc.19/instructions.md:1555:const user = await db.orm.users.create({ name: 'Alice' });\nnode_modules/@prisma/orm-postgres/skills/prisma-8/upgrading/app/upgrades/8.0.0-rc.18-to-8.0.0-rc.19/instructions.md:1557:const posts = await db.orm.posts.where({ authorId: user.id }).include('author').all();\nnode_modules/@prisma/orm-postgres/skills/prisma-8/upgrading/app/upgrades/8.0.0-rc.20-to-8.0.0-rc.21/instructions.md:501:The same text is read inside an `.include()`. `infinity` and `-infinity` are unchanged. The generated `now()` values of these columns, such as `temporal.UpdatedAtString`, are written in the same form.\nnode_modules/@prisma/orm-postgres/skills/prisma-8/upgrading/app/upgrades/8.0.0-rc.13-to-8.0.0-rc.14/instructions.md:70:      Mongo reads now decode included documents (`include(...)`) and composite-type (value\nnode_modules/@prisma/orm-postgres/skills/prisma-8/upgrading/app/upgrades/8.0.0-rc.14-to-8.0.0-rc.15/instructions.md:158:      A `char(n)` column now reads the same through `.include()` as through a flat read: without the trailing spaces that pad it, where an include used to return them, and keeping a trailing tab or newline, which a flat read used to drop. Compare `char` values without their padding.\nnode_modules/@prisma/orm-postgres/skills/prisma-8/upgrading/app/upgrades/8.0.0-rc.14-to-8.0.0-rc.15/instructions.md:172:      On SQLite, an `.include()` of a row whose `sql/int@1` column holds an INTEGER past 2^53, or a REAL, now throws `RUNTIME.DECODE_FAILED`, where it read the value rounded or with a fraction. Store such values in a `BigInt` or `Float` column.\nnode_modules/@prisma/orm-postgres/skills/prisma-8/upgrading/app/upgrades/8.0.0-rc.14-to-8.0.0-rc.15/instructions.md:666:A `contract.json` with such a default, emitted by an earlier version or edited by hand, still loads. `db init`, `db update` and `migration plan` used to plan the default; they now stop with `CONTRACT.DEFAULT_INVALID`, which names the table, the column, the codec and the value. Emit the contract again with this version, and correct the default in the contract source if emit refuses it. Running a `migration.ts` that an earlier version planned with such a default fails with the same message; correct the default in that file. Every statement that writes a default reads it this way: a new table or column, a changed default, and on SQLite a rebuilt table, which used to write a changed default or a rebuilt table's defaults unread. Each element of a list default is read the same way, and the message names the element's position: `Column \"post\".\"tags\" has a default (element 2) its codec pg/text@1 refuses: pg/text@1 JSON value must be a string`. A NULL element stays NULL. The codecs that carry PostgreSQL's own date and time text, those of `DateString`, `TimeString`, `TimestampString`, `TimestamptzString` and `Timetz`, read only a date or time in ISO 8601 or as PostgreSQL writes it, so such a default as `'now'`, which PostgreSQL would read once when it creates the table, is refused the same way. An `.include()` of such a column reads the text PostgreSQL writes in the ISO DateStyle, its default; on a server set to another DateStyle it now fails with `RUNTIME.DECODE_FAILED`, where it passed the text through. Set `DateStyle` to `ISO` on that server.\nnode_modules/@prisma/orm-postgres/skills/prisma-8/upgrading/app/upgrades/8.0.0-rc.14-to-8.0.0-rc.15/instructions.md:741:`pg/text-array@1`, the codec of a contract-free `textArray()` column, reads a `text[]` column's NULL elements as `null`, so its application type is `readonly (string | null)[]` where it was `readonly string[]`. Code typed by a `textArray()` column, or by `min` or `max` over one, sees `string | null` elements; handle the `null`. Read through an `.include()`, a two-dimensional `text[]` value now throws `RUNTIME.DECODE_FAILED`, where it read as the text `\"a,b\"`.\nnode_modules/@prisma/orm-postgres/skills/prisma-8/upgrading/app/upgrades/8.0.0-rc.14-to-8.0.0-rc.15/instructions.md:745:PostgreSQL pads a `char(n)` value with spaces to its length: `'a'` in a `char(3)` column is stored as `'a  '`. A flat read dropped every trailing whitespace character, so a stored `'a\\t'` also read as `\"a\"`, while `.include()` returned the padded text, `\"a  \"`. Both reads now return the value without the padding and nothing more: `\"a\"` for `'a'`, and `\"a\\t\"` for `'a\\t'`. On SQLite, which does not pad, both reads drop trailing spaces, as a flat read did. Code that compared an included `char` value with its padding, or relied on a flat read dropping a trailing tab or newline, compares the value without its padding.\nnode_modules/@prisma/orm-postgres/skills/prisma-8/upgrading/app/upgrades/8.0.0-rc.14-to-8.0.0-rc.15/instructions.md:753:`sql/int@1` holds a JavaScript safe integer. On SQLite, an INTEGER column can hold a larger integer or a REAL. An `.include()` read such a value rounded or with a fraction; it now throws `RUNTIME.DECODE_FAILED`, naming the codec. A flat read is unchanged. Store an integer past 2^53 in a `BigInt` column and a fraction in a `Float` column.\nnode_modules/@prisma/orm-postgres/skills/prisma-8/upgrading/app/upgrades/8.0.0-rc.14-to-8.0.0-rc.15/instructions.md:990:`where`, `orderBy`, `limit`, `offset`, `distinct`, `distinctOn`, `cursor` and `include` now return the collection they were called on, with what they establish added to its type as a fact. A custom collection class keeps its methods through the chain, so `db.Post.where({ userId }).withTitle('orm')` and `db.Post.include('user').withTitle('orm')` compile.\nnode_modules/@prisma/orm-postgres/skills/prisma-8/upgrading/app/upgrades/8.0.0-rc.14-to-8.0.0-rc.15/instructions.md:1027:The exception is `update` with a relation callback, such as `posts: (posts) => posts.connect(...)`. It finds its row by the filter alone and ignored the order, the limit, the offset, the cursor, `distinct` and `distinctOn`, so it could change another row than the chain chose. It now throws `ORM.ARGUMENT_INVALID` on a collection with any of them. Find the row first and filter to it:\nnode_modules/@prisma/orm-postgres/skills/prisma-8/upgrading/app/upgrades/8.0.0-rc.14-to-8.0.0-rc.15/instructions.md:1030:- await users.where({ teamId }).orderBy((u) => u.createdAt.asc()).update({ posts: (posts) => posts.connect([{ id: postId }]) });\nnode_modules/@prisma/orm-postgres/skills/prisma-8/upgrading/app/upgrades/8.0.0-rc.14-to-8.0.0-rc.15/instructions.md:1032:+ if (oldest) await users.where({ id: oldest.id }).update({ posts: (posts) => posts.connect([{ id: postId }]) });\nnode_modules/@prisma/orm-postgres/skills/prisma-8/upgrading/app/upgrades/8.0.0-rc.14-to-8.0.0-rc.15/instructions.md:1160:+ const withUser = posts.include('user');\nnode_modules/@prisma/orm-postgres/skills/prisma-8/upgrading/app/upgrades/8.0.0-rc.1-to-8.0.0-rc.2/instructions.md:102:      `aggregate()`, `groupBy().aggregate()`, `groupBy().having()`, the `include(...)` reducers,\nnode_modules/@prisma/orm-postgres/skills/prisma-8/upgrading/app/upgrades/8.0.0-rc.1-to-8.0.0-rc.2/instructions.md:142:      `RUNTIME.DECODE_FAILED` instead of returning a rounded number — on the `.include()` path as\nnode_modules/@prisma/orm-postgres/skills/prisma-8/upgrading/app/upgrades/8.0.0-rc.1-to-8.0.0-rc.2/instructions.md:163:        - \".include(\"\nnode_modules/@prisma/orm-postgres/skills/prisma-8/upgrading/app/upgrades/8.0.0-rc.1-to-8.0.0-rc.2/instructions.md:289:| `db.orm.User.include('posts', (posts) => posts.count())` | one reducer per declared operation |\nnode_modules/@prisma/orm-postgres/skills/prisma-8/upgrading/app/upgrades/8.0.0-rc.1-to-8.0.0-rc.2/instructions.md:406:That is the trade these defaults make: a value you can compare, serialise, and do arithmetic with, and a loud failure rather than a quietly wrong total. It fires on the `.include()` path too — the reducer's value travels as a JSON number, but the guard runs after the parse, and rounding is monotone, so a value that was outside the range is still outside it after parsing.\nnode_modules/@prisma/orm-postgres/skills/prisma-8/upgrading/app/upgrades/0.17-to-8.0.0-rc.1/instructions.md:15:      (`.include('posts', (p) => p.count())`) decode the same way, so an included count is a\nnode_modules/@prisma/orm-postgres/skills/prisma-8/upgrading/app/upgrades/0.17-to-8.0.0-rc.1/instructions.md:55:Include reducers follow the same rules — `.include('posts', (posts) => posts.count())` yields a `bigint`, and a parent with no related rows reads `0n` where it read `0`.\nnode_modules/@prisma/orm-postgres/skills/prisma-8/upgrading/app/upgrades/8.0.0-rc.22-to-8.0.0-rc.23/instructions.md:93:      A MongoDB migration creates and drops each index by its name. `prisma migration plan` writes the name into `migration.ts`, as in `createIndex(\"users\", [{ field: \"email\", direction: 1 }], { name: \"User_email_key\", unique: true })` and `dropIndex(\"users\", [{ field: \"email\", direction: 1 }], { name: \"User_email_key\", unique: true })`. The operation id is `index.<collection>.<create|drop>(<name>)`, such as `index.users.create(email_1)`, where it was `index.users.create(email:1)`. A `migration.ts` written by an earlier release keeps working: an index without a name is named after its keys, as before.\nnode_modules/@prisma/orm-postgres/skills/prisma-8/upgrading/app/upgrades/8.0.0-rc.22-to-8.0.0-rc.23/instructions.md:414:A script or test that matches an operation id, such as `index.users.create(email:1)`, matches the name instead: `index.users.create(email_1)`.\nnode_modules/@prisma/orm-postgres/skills/prisma-8/references/runtime.md:78:const created = await db.orm.public.User.create({ email: 'alice@example.com', name: 'Alice' });\nnode_modules/@prisma/orm-postgres/skills/prisma-8/references/runtime.md:166:The rule to remember: inside a request, `db` does everything the `db` from `postgres()` does, so any documented `db.orm...`, `db.sql...`, `db.raw...`, `db.transaction(...)`, `db.prepare(...)` or `db.query(...)` snippet works unchanged. Await every query before the `await using` scope ends. The connection's `close()` waits until the runtime has been idle for one turn of the event loop (a `setTimeout(0)`), then refuses new work. A lazy ORM read such as `db.orm.public.User.all()`, or `db.query(plan)`, returned from the scope without `await` starts only when the caller awaits it, after the scope has closed, so it rejects with `DRIVER.NOT_CONNECTED` (\"Runtime is closed\"), whose `fix` names the missing `await`. Work that keeps the runtime busy from the close onward completes: a `first()`, `execute()` or `transaction()`, an ORM write and its reload, including `include(...).create()` and a nested `create()`, and an async helper whose steps go through `db.orm`, `db.transaction()` or `db.prepare()`; a transaction commits. Such work is never refused because of the close; if it fails for its own reason, it rejects like any unawaited promise. A helper that calls `db.runtime()` after the scope has ended throws \"Postgres connection is closed\" at that call. Work that starts after the runtime was idle for a turn is refused. Whether a query sent after waiting on something other than the database counts as idle depends on when that callback runs relative to the timer, so do not rely on it. An unawaited promise that fails then, or fails for its own reason, rejects like any unawaited promise, and Node reports it as unhandled. Write `return await db.orm...`, not `return db.orm...`.\nnode_modules/@prisma/orm-postgres/skills/prisma-8/references/runtime.md:364:  const user = await tx.orm.public.User.create({ email: 'alice@example.com' });\nnode_modules/@prisma/orm-postgres/skills/prisma-8/references/runtime.md:365:  await tx.orm.public.Post.create({ userId: user.id, title: 'hello' });\nnode_modules/@prisma/orm-postgres/skills/prisma-8/references/queries-mongo.md:21:| Eager-load a reference relation | **ORM `.include('<relation>')`** | Lowers to `$lookup`; composes with `.where` / `.select` / `.orderBy` / `.limit`. |\nnode_modules/@prisma/orm-postgres/skills/prisma-8/references/queries-mongo.md:71:Mongo reference relations eager-load through the same `.include('<relation>')` surface; the ORM lowers to `$lookup`:\nnode_modules/@prisma/orm-postgres/skills/prisma-8/references/queries-mongo.md:75:  .include('author')\nnode_modules/@prisma/orm-postgres/skills/prisma-8/references/queries-mongo.md:89:const user = await db.orm.users.create({\nnode_modules/@prisma/orm-postgres/skills/prisma-8/upgrading/extension/upgrades/0.15-to-0.16/instructions.md:25:      `contract infer`'s back-relation field name generation used a hand-rolled pluralization rule that appended `es` to any table name already ending in `s`/`x`/`z`/`ch`/`sh`, doubling an already-plural table name (`sessions` -> `sessionses`). `contract infer` now uses real inflection (the `pluralize` library) and produces the correct name (`sessions` stays `sessions`; a genuinely singular `status` still becomes `statuses`). This only affects a future `contract infer` run — an already-generated `.prisma` file is untouched, so nothing breaks until you next re-run infer for your pack. If you do re-run `contract infer` against a schema with an already-plural table name, diff the regenerated `.prisma` file for any back-relation field whose name changed — that's a public field name your pack's consumers access via `.include()`/`.select()`/the generated TypeScript types, so a rename is a breaking change to your pack's own published surface, to be versioned and documented like any other.\nnode_modules/@prisma/orm-postgres/skills/prisma-8/references/queries.md:116:  await db.orm.public.User.create(u);\nnode_modules/@prisma/orm-postgres/skills/prisma-8/references/queries.md:121://   await db.orm.users.create(u);\nnode_modules/@prisma/orm-postgres/skills/prisma-8/references/queries.md:177:- `ResultType<typeof query>` — the row of any ORM collection value (plain, `.include()`, `.select()`, `.variant()`), and of SQL lane plans. Bind the query to a name first; `typeof` needs a value. `.variant()` takes the variant's discriminator value, not its model name: `db.orm.public.Task.variant('bug')` for `@@base(Task, \"bug\")`. Call it once, on the base collection; a second `.variant()` on a variant collection is refused.\nnode_modules/@prisma/orm-postgres/skills/prisma-8/references/queries.md:187:const usersWithTasks = () => db.orm.public.User.include('tasks');\nnode_modules/@prisma/orm-postgres/skills/prisma-8/references/queries.md:194:type UserResponse = Shape<Models.public_User, { '-': 'email'; posts: { '+': 'id' | 'title' | 'tags' } }>;\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
